### Working Group Rechartering & Open Pull Requests
*   Aaron Parecki and Danny Zollner led the charter discussion using [Chair Slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-scim-chair-slides-00), [Charter Draft v1 (Paolo)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-scim-charter-draft-v1-paolo-00), and [SCIM WG Draft Charter - Open PRs](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-scim-scim-wg-draft-charter-open-prs-00).
*   **Session Poll**:
    *   *Who has read the proposed charter text?* — yes: 5, no: 17, no_opinion: 0 (total: 36)
*   **Open PR Review**:
    *   *PR 7*: Replaces the term "account" with "resource" across the text for terminology consistency.
    *   *PR 3 / PR 9*: Expands scope to Non-Human Identities (NHI), workload identities, software agents, and separates physical devices from software principals.
    *   *Large Multivalued Attributes*: Clarifies that both retrieval and modification/bulk operations at scale are in scope.
    *   *PR 12 (Schema Semantics)*: Focuses on improving complex attribute definitions and schema rules. Hirsch Singhal suggested using "strictly defined" or "less ambiguous" rather than "clearer".
    *   *Filtering Discovery*: Adds explicit discovery of supported filtering operators per attribute.
    *   *PR 13 (Roles & Entitlements)*: Expands scope to cover discovery and enumeration of available roles/entitlements. Hirsch Singhal noted requests for client-side entitlement creation; Danny Zollner clarified that role definitions remain read-only while assignments are updatable.
    *   *Asynchronous Processing*: Adds scope for defining standard behaviors for asynchronous request/response processing over HTTP.
    *   *Flat-File / Schematized Ingestion*: Proposes defining non-semantic bulk schematized data ingestion. Justin Richer warned that the wording could be misinterpreted as creating a new non-SCIM protocol and recommended framing the work strictly around SCIM schemas as standalone artifacts.
*   **AD Feedback**: Responsible AD Chris Lemmons reminded the group that charters should remain high-level, noting that existing SCIM RFCs provide natural bounding boxes without needing overly prescriptive text.

